4040 0 obj
<</Title(PHISHING - WHEN DECEPTION LANDS IN YOUR INBOX)/Dest[29 0 R/Fit]/First 4041 0 R/Last 4052 0 R/Count -7/Prev 4033 0 R/Parent 3931 0 R/Next 4053 0 R>>
endobj

4041 0 obj
<</Title(One email. One convincing story. One click that could expose an entire organisation.)/Dest[29 0 R/Fit]/Parent 4040 0 R/Next 4042 0 R>>
endobj

4042 0 obj
<</Title(An email arrives on a busy morning from your bank, asking you to verify your account, or a supplier shares an updated invoice. Or your manager requests a confidential file before an important meeting.)/Dest[29 0 R/Fit]/Prev 4041 0 R/Parent 4040 0 R/Next 4043 0 R>>
endobj

4043 0 obj
<</Title(Everything looks familiar. The sender\220s name seems right. You have dozens of tasks waiting, so you act without giving it much thought.)/Dest[29 0 R/Fit]/First 4044 0 R/Last 4044 0 R/Count -1/Prev 4042 0 R/Parent 4040 0 R/Next 4045 0 R>>
endobj

4044 0 obj
<</Title(But Wait! What if the sender is an impostor?)/Dest[29 0 R/Fit]/Parent 4043 0 R>>
endobj

4045 0 obj
<</Title(Welcome to phishing, a form of social engineering in which attackers disguise themselves as trusted people or organisations to trick recipients into revealing sensitive information, clicking malicious links, opening harmful attachments, or authorising fraudulent actions.)/Dest[29 0 R/Fit]/Prev 4043 0 R/Parent 4040 0 R/Next 4046 0 R>>
endobj

4046 0 obj
<</Title(The objective may be to steal login credentials, gain access to business systems, obtain financial information, or set the stage for a larger fraud. And the most convincing phishing attempts do not always look suspicious. They look like ordinary work.)/Dest[29 0 R/Fit]/First 4047 0 R/Last 4050 0 R/Count -4/Prev 4045 0 R/Parent 4040 0 R/Next 4051 0 R>>
endobj

4047 0 obj
<</Title(How Does a Phishing Attack Actually Work?)/Dest[29 0 R/Fit]/Parent 4046 0 R/Next 4048 0 R>>
endobj

4048 0 obj
<</Title(Step 1: Earn your trust.)/Dest[29 0 R/Fit]/Prev 4047 0 R/Parent 4046 0 R/Next 4049 0 R>>
endobj

4049 0 obj
<</Title(Step 2: Give you a reason to act.)/Dest[29 0 R/Fit]/Prev 4048 0 R/Parent 4046 0 R/Next 4050 0 R>>
endobj

4050 0 obj
<</Title(Step 3: Get you to do something.)/Dest[29 0 R/Fit]/Prev 4049 0 R/Parent 4046 0 R>>
endobj

4051 0 obj
<</Title(Step 4: Turn that action into an opportunity.)/Dest[30 0 R/Fit]/Prev 4046 0 R/Parent 4040 0 R/Next 4052 0 R>>
endobj

4052 0 obj
<</Title(Step 5: Use the access or trust gained.)/Dest[30 0 R/Fit]/Prev 4051 0 R/Parent 4040 0 R>>
endobj

4053 0 obj
<</Title(Let\220s Burst the Biggest Myth!)/Dest[30 0 R/Fit]/Prev 4040 0 R/Parent 3931 0 R/Next 4054 0 R>>
endobj

4054 0 obj
<</Title(Let us dig deeper into this myth.)/Dest[30 0 R/Fit]/Prev 4053 0 R/Parent 3931 0 R/Next 4055 0 R>>
endobj

4055 0 obj
<</Title(Modern Ways of Phishing?)/Dest[30 0 R/Fit]/First 4056 0 R/Last 4056 0 R/Count -1/Prev 4054 0 R/Parent 3931 0 R/Next 4057 0 R>>
endobj

4056 0 obj
<</Title(01. Smishing = SMS + Phishing)/Dest[30 0 R/Fit]/Parent 4055 0 R>>
endobj

4057 0 obj
<</Title(02. Quishing: QR Code + Phishing)/Dest[31 0 R/Fit]/Prev 4055 0 R/Parent 3931 0 R/Next 4058 0 R>>
endobj

4058 0 obj
<</Title(03. Vishing = Voice Call + Phishing)/Dest[31 0 R/Fit]/Prev 4057 0 R/Parent 3931 0 R/Next 4059 0 R>>
endobj

4059 0 obj
<</Title(The Defence System: Stop the Message, Protect the Account, Respond Quickly)/Dest[31 0 R/Fit]/Prev 4058 0 R/Parent 3931 0 R/Next 4060 0 R>>
endobj

4060 0 obj
<</Title(Layer 1: Stop Suspicious Messages Before They Reach People)/Dest[31 0 R/Fit]/First 4061 0 R/Last 4061 0 R/Count -1/Prev 4059 0 R/Parent 3931 0 R/Next 4062 0 R>>
endobj

4061 0 obj
<</Title(Organisations can also use email authentication methods such as SPF, DKIM and DMARC. In simple terms, these help receiving systems check whether an email is authorised to use a particular sending domain and whether it shows signs of tampering. When configured properly, they can reduce certain types of sender impersonation.)/Dest[32 0 R/Fit]/Parent 4060 0 R>>
endobj

4062 0 obj
<</Title(Layer 2: Make Sure One Click Does Not Open Every Door)/Dest[32 0 R/Fit]/First 4063 0 R/Last 4068 0 R/Count -6/Prev 4060 0 R/Parent 3931 0 R/Next 4069 0 R>>
endobj

4063 0 obj
<</Title(Even the best email filters can miss a threat. That makes account security the next important layer.)/Dest[32 0 R/Fit]/Parent 4062 0 R/Next 4064 0 R>>
endobj

4064 0 obj
<</Title(Organisations should use Multi-Factor Authentication \(MFA\), which requires an additional step beyond a password when signing in. Where possible, they should consider phishing-resistant options such as passkeys or security keys, which are designed to prevent credentials from being used on fake websites.)/Dest[32 0 R/Fit]/Prev 4063 0 R/Parent 4062 0 R/Next 4065 0 R>>
endobj

4065 0 obj
<</Title(Access should be limited to what people need for their roles, so that one compromised account does not automatically provide access to everything.)/Dest[32 0 R/Fit]/Prev 4064 0 R/Parent 4062 0 R/Next 4066 0 R>>
endobj

4066 0 obj
<</Title(MFA is valuable, but not all forms offer the same level of protection against every phishing technique. Some attacks can trick users into)/Dest[32 0 R/Fit]/Prev 4065 0 R/Parent 4062 0 R/Next 4067 0 R>>
endobj

4067 0 obj
<</Title(approving a sign-in or capture an active login session.)/Dest[32 0 R/Fit]/Prev 4066 0 R/Parent 4062 0 R/Next 4068 0 R>>
endobj

4068 0 obj
<</Title(The goal is simply to make it harder for a stolen password or compromised account to become a route into the wider organisation.)/Dest[32 0 R/Fit]/Prev 4067 0 R/Parent 4062 0 R>>
endobj

4069 0 obj
<</Title(Layer 3: Make Reporting and Response Easy)/Dest[32 0 R/Fit]/First 4070 0 R/Last 4072 0 R/Count -3/Prev 4062 0 R/Parent 3931 0 R/Next 4073 0 R>>
endobj

4070 0 obj
<</Title(Suppose an employee realises they have clicked a suspicious link or shared their credentials. They should not have to search through policy documents to find out what to do.)/Dest[32 0 R/Fit]/Parent 4069 0 R/Next 4071 0 R>>
endobj

4071 0 obj
<</Title(A simple phishing-reporting button, clear instructions and a supportive reporting culture can help employees raise concerns quickly. The security team should be able to investigate the message, remove it from other inboxes where possible, check for suspicious account activity and take steps to contain any compromise.)/Dest[32 0 R/Fit]/Prev 4070 0 R/Parent 4069 0 R/Next 4072 0 R>>
endobj

4072 0 obj
<</Title(Importantly, reporting should not depend on a suspicious message containing a link or attachment. An employee who receives a fraudulent payment request or a suspicious message asking them to call a number should still be able to report it.)/Dest[32 0 R/Fit]/Prev 4071 0 R/Parent 4069 0 R>>
endobj

4073 0 obj
<</Title(Speed matters because the earlier an organisation knows about an attempted attack, the sooner it can investigate and limit the damage.)/Dest[32 0 R/Fit]/Prev 4069 0 R/Parent 3931 0 R/Next 4074 0 R>>
endobj

4074 0 obj
<</Title(Train People and Strengthen the Systems Around Them)/Dest[32 0 R/Fit]/First 4075 0 R/Last 4077 0 R/Count -3/Prev 4073 0 R/Parent 3931 0 R/Next 4078 0 R>>
endobj

4075 0 obj
<</Title(Technology can block many threats, but it cannot make every decision for an employee. Effective training should use situations people may actually face: an unexpected refund message, a QR code requesting a login, a call claiming to be from the bank, or an urgent request from the CEO.)/Dest[32 0 R/Fit]/Parent 4074 0 R/Next 4076 0 R>>
endobj

4076 0 obj
<</Title(Training also needs to stay current and regular refreshers and lessons drawn from real incidents can keep the guidance relevant.)/Dest[32 0 R/Fit]/Prev 4075 0 R/Parent 4074 0 R/Next 4077 0 R>>
endobj

4077 0 obj
<</Title(Alongside training, IT teams use security tools to watch for suspicious activity across the organisation\220s network and systems.)/Dest[32 0 R/Fit]/Prev 4076 0 R/Parent 4074 0 R>>
endobj

4078 0 obj
<</Title(Two more points make training work:)/Dest[32 0 R/Fit]/First 4079 0 R/Last 4084 0 R/Count -5/Prev 4074 0 R/Parent 3931 0 R/Next 4085 0 R>>
endobj

4079 0 obj
<</Title(Reward reporting: Someone who is scolded for clicking will hide the next click. The hidden click is the one that becomes a breach.)/Dest[32 0 R/Fit]/Parent 4078 0 R/Next 4080 0 R>>
endobj

4080 0 obj
<</Title(Train the IT team too: Run practice drills where an alert fires and the team must investigate, block and sign users out within minutes.)/Dest[32 0 R/Fit]/Prev 4079 0 R/Parent 4078 0 R/Next 4081 0 R>>
endobj

4081 0 obj
<</Title(An Intrusion Detection System \(IDS\) monitors activity and alerts the security team when it identifies signs of a possible attack. An Intrusion Prevention System \(IPS\) can take action to block certain suspicious traffic. Depending on how they are configured, these tools may help detect or stop activity linked to malicious connections, malware or attempts to access systems without permission.)/Dest[32 0 R/Fit]/First 4082 0 R/Last 4082 0 R/Count -1/Prev 4080 0 R/Parent 4078 0 R/Next 4083 0 R>>
